# ─── Server ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
PORT=5000
NODE_ENV=development

# ─── Database ───────────────────────────────────────────────
DATABASE_URL=mongodb://localhost:27017/sabbir-book

# ─── Auth ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
BCRYPT_SALT_ROUNDS=12
JWT_ACCESS_SECRET=replace_with_a_strong_random_secret
JWT_ACCESS_EXPIRES_IN=15m
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=replace_with_a_strong_random_secret
JWT_REFRESH_EXPIRES_IN=7d

# Max concurrent logged-in devices per user (oldest auto-evicted on a new login)
DEVICE_LIMIT=2

# ─── Sign in with Google ────────────────────────────────────
#
# WHERE TO GET THESE
#   1. https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials (pick or create a project)
#   2. "Create credentials" → "OAuth client ID" → Application type: "Web application"
#   3. Under "Authorised JavaScript origins" add every origin the site is served
#      from — http://localhost:3001 for dev, plus the production URL. Google's
#      button silently refuses to render on an origin that is not listed.
#   4. Copy the "Client ID" (it looks like 1234567890-abc123.apps.googleusercontent.com)
#
# THE AUDIENCE RULE — the one thing that has to be right
#   The browser asks Google for a token minted FOR a specific client id: the one
#   in the CLIENT's NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID. The server then refuses any
#   token whose `aud` claim is not one of the ids below. So the client's id MUST
#   appear here, or every sign-in fails with the same vague 401 a forged token
#   gets — there is no friendlier error, by design.
#   In the normal single-project setup these two are literally the same string.
#
# Leave GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID blank to switch the feature off: the server answers
# 503 "not configured" on /api/user/google-signin and nothing crashes at boot.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
#
# OPTIONAL second accepted audience. Only needed when more than one OAuth client
# legitimately signs users into this backend — a separate Android/iOS client, or
# a migration between Cloud projects (the digits before the '-' are the project
# number, so ids from different projects differ there).
#
# ⚠ Every id listed is a party TRUSTED TO MINT LOGINS for this site. Whoever
#   controls that OAuth client can sign in as any of their users. Only ever put
#   a client id you own here, and delete it once the migration is done. The
#   server logs a warning at startup when the two ids are from different projects.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID_ALT=
#
# Only needed for a server-side authorization-code exchange, which this flow
# does NOT do (the browser sends an ID token straight from Google Identity
# Services). Safe to leave blank.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ─── Cloudinary ─────────────────────────────────────────────
CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME=
CLOUDINARY_API_KEY=
CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET=

# ─── Payment gateways ───────────────────────────────────────
# Leave EVERY key below blank to keep the shop exactly as it is today: buyers get
# cash-on-delivery plus the manual Send-Money flow (they send to the wallet
# numbers set in Admin → Settings and type the TrxID, an admin verifies it).
#
# A gateway switches itself on only when ALL of its keys are filled. A partially
# filled block stays OFF and logs a warning at boot — that is deliberate, because
# a half-configured gateway would otherwise fail in front of a paying buyer.
# Check what the server thinks is live with:  GET /api/payment/gateways

# SERVER_URL — this API's own PUBLIC base URL, no trailing slash.
# Both gateways redirect the buyer and post their IPN back to URLs built from
# this, so it must be reachable from the public internet — 'localhost' works only
# for local testing (use a tunnel such as ngrok to test callbacks properly).
#   dev  : http://localhost:5000
#   prod : https://api.yourdomain.com
# MUST be a real domain, NOT a host with a raw IP in it. SSLCommerz's WAF rejects
# a session request whose callback URLs contain an IP address ("Request Rejected
# ... support ID") — so Coolify's default *.<ip>.sslip.io host does NOT work here.
# Point a clean domain at this API (e.g. api.yourdomain.com), or reuse the client
# domain if it proxies /api to this server (SERVER_URL=https://yourdomain.com).
SERVER_URL=http://localhost:5000

# ─── SSLCommerz ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Aggregator: one integration covers bKash, Nagad, Rocket, cards and net banking.
# Where to get these: register a merchant account at https://sslcommerz.com and
# read them off the Merchant Panel → My Stores. Sandbox credentials are issued
# instantly and for free at https://developer.sslcommerz.com/registration/ —
# trade-licence paperwork is only needed for the live store.
# Sandbox vs live: they are DIFFERENT credential pairs, and the only switch is
# SSLCOMMERZ_IS_LIVE. false → sandbox.sslcommerz.com (test money, test cards);
# true → securepay.sslcommerz.com (real money). A sandbox store id sent to the
# live host is rejected, so never flip the flag without swapping the keys too.
SSLCOMMERZ_STORE_ID=
SSLCOMMERZ_STORE_PASS=
SSLCOMMERZ_IS_LIVE=false

# ─── bKash (direct, Tokenized Checkout / PGW) ───────────────
# Direct bKash-only integration — lower per-transaction cost than the aggregator,
# but it is bKash and nothing else, and onboarding is slower.
# Where to get these: apply for a bKash Merchant/PGW account through bKash
# business support; on approval they send an onboarding pack containing all four
# values. Sandbox credentials come from https://developer.bka.sh (register an
# app) and are separate from the live ones.
# Sandbox vs live: BKASH_IS_LIVE alone picks the host.
#   false → https://tokenized.sandbox.bka.sh   (test wallets)
#   true  → https://tokenized.pay.bka.sh       (real money)
# Token, create, execute and query all follow that one flag, so sandbox keys can
# never leak into a live call.
BKASH_APP_KEY=
BKASH_APP_SECRET=
BKASH_USERNAME=
BKASH_PASSWORD=
BKASH_IS_LIVE=false
# Optional. Leave blank unless bKash gave you a non-standard grant-token
# endpoint; the correct URL for the host above is derived automatically.
BKASH_GRANT_TOKEN_URL=

# ─── Email (Gmail SMTP) ─────────────────────────────────────
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=
MAIL_FROM=
MAIL_FROM_NAME=Sabbir Book
SENDGRID_API_KEY=

# ─── SMS: MiMSMS (sms.mimsms.com) ───────────────────────────
#
# Leave SMS_API_KEY blank and the SMS service stays in demo mode: every message
# is logged to the console and nothing is sent. Fill it in and real texts start
# going — no code change, no redeploy of anything but the environment.
#
# Two things bite on the panel side and neither looks like a bug from here:
#   1. the key has to be ACTIVATED in the panel's Developer menu after it is
#      created — a fresh key is refused until then
#   2. this server's public IP has to be whitelisted there. A Coolify redeploy
#      can change it, and the symptom is texts silently stopping.
# The server logs which of these to check on its first send of each boot.
#
# SMS_USERNAME is the email the shop signs into the panel with — MiMSMS has no
# separate API username.
SMS_USERNAME=
SMS_API_KEY=
# The sender the pack was bought under. A non-masking pack is a numeric sender,
# a masking pack is the brand name. Copy it exactly from the panel.
SMS_SENDER_ID=
# 'T' = transactional, which is all this shop sends (orders and accounts).
SMS_TRANSACTION_TYPE=T
# Only set this if MiMSMS changes the path; the default is the current one.
SMS_ENDPOINT=https://api.mimsms.com/api/SmsSending/OneToMany

# ─── Order alerts: Telegram + WhatsApp ──────────────────────
# Fires when a buyer places a book order (POST /api/orders).
#
# ALL OPTIONAL. Every key below can stay blank: the server logs "unconfigured —
# skipping" and the in-app notification bell still works. A missing key never
# crashes anything and never fails an order.
#
# These live in .env and NOT in site settings on purpose — GET /api/settings is
# public and unauthenticated, so a bot token stored there would be world-readable.

# Name used to sign off buyer-facing messages.
SHOP_NAME=Sabbir Books

# ── Telegram (admin only) ───────────────────────────────────
# Easiest channel to turn on: two values, no business verification, no fees.
#
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN
#   1. Open Telegram, message @BotFather
#   2. /newbot → give it a name and a username ending in "bot"
#   3. BotFather replies with a token like 123456789:AAE...  ← paste it here
#
# TELEGRAM_ADMIN_CHAT_ID — the numeric id of the chat the bot should post into.
#   1. Send any message to YOUR new bot (a bot cannot start a conversation)
#   2. Open https://api.telegram.org/bot<TOKEN>/getUpdates in a browser
#   3. Copy result[0].message.chat.id  (e.g. 987654321)
#   For a staff group: add the bot to the group, post a message there, then read
#   the same URL — group ids are negative, e.g. -1001234567890.
#   Comma-separate to notify several chats: 987654321,-1001234567890
#
# NOTE: buyers CANNOT be reached on Telegram. A bot may only message someone who
# has already started it, and no API turns a phone number into a chat id. The
# buyer channel is WhatsApp.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
TELEGRAM_ADMIN_CHAT_ID=

# ── WhatsApp (Meta Cloud API — buyer and admin) ─────────────
# Setup at https://developers.facebook.com → create an app → add the "WhatsApp"
# product. Needs a Meta Business account and a phone number you do not already
# use in the WhatsApp phone app.
#
# WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID  WhatsApp → API Setup → "Phone number ID" (a number,
#                           NOT the phone number itself)
# WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN     Same page. The temporary token there EXPIRES IN 24
#                           HOURS — fine for a first test, useless in production.
#                           For a permanent one: Business Settings → System users
#                           → add a system user → Generate token → select the app
#                           → scopes whatsapp_business_messaging +
#                           whatsapp_business_management.
# WHATSAPP_ADMIN_TO         Your own WhatsApp number in international form with
#                           no + and no leading 0, e.g. 8801712345678. Leave
#                           blank to skip the admin WhatsApp copy (Telegram
#                           already covers the admin).
WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN=
WHATSAPP_ADMIN_TO=
WHATSAPP_API_VERSION=v21.0
WHATSAPP_DEFAULT_COUNTRY_CODE=880

# ── WhatsApp message templates (REQUIRED for WhatsApp to work) ──
# A business may only send free-text WhatsApp inside a 24-hour window that opens
# when the customer messages the business first. A buyer checking out on the
# website has not, so the ONLY thing WhatsApp will deliver is a template Meta
# approved in advance. Free text is rejected with error 131047.
#
# Register these at business.facebook.com → WhatsApp Manager → Message templates
# → Create template. Category UTILITY, language Bengali (bn). Approval is
# usually minutes. Body text to paste, EXACTLY seven variables in this order:
#
#   {{1}} buyer name   {{2}} order number    {{3}} book list
#   {{4}} delivery charge   {{5}} grand total   {{6}} payment method
#   {{7}} address and phone
#
# order_received_bn (to the buyer) —
#   আসসালামু আলাইকুম {{1}}, আপনার অর্ডারটি আমরা পেয়েছি। অর্ডার নম্বর: {{2}}।
#   বই: {{3}}। ডেলিভারি চার্জ: {{4}} টাকা। সর্বমোট: {{5}} টাকা।
#   পেমেন্ট: {{6}}। ডেলিভারি ঠিকানা ও ফোন: {{7}}।
#   আমরা শীঘ্রই আপনার সাথে যোগাযোগ করব। ধন্যবাদ।
#
# new_order_admin_bn (to the admin) —
#   নতুন অর্ডার এসেছে। ক্রেতা: {{1}}। অর্ডার নম্বর: {{2}}। বই: {{3}}।
#   ডেলিভারি চার্জ: {{4}} টাকা। সর্বমোট: {{5}} টাকা। পেমেন্ট: {{6}}।
#   ঠিকানা ও ফোন: {{7}}।
#
# Meta needs a sample value for each variable at submission time. If you name the
# templates differently, change the two keys below to match.
WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_ORDER_BUYER=order_received_bn
WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_ORDER_ADMIN=new_order_admin_bn
WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_LANG=bn

# ─── Client ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
CLIENT_URL=http://localhost:3000
